window.docContents[126].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Theorem-files/Symbolic.Theorems.ModelSecurity/#file-Symbolic___Theorems___ModelSecurity":{"contents":"Paper › Symbolic/Theorems › ModelSecurity.leanTier 1 · theorem file\n\nTier 1 · theorem file used by the symbolic proof 10 headline theorems 124 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. This file states headline theorems. Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\nCited in the paper* Table 2, Forward secrecy: forward_secrecy* Table 2, Post-compromise security: post_compromise_security* Table 2, Initiator secrecy: alice_initiator_secrecy* Table 2, Responder secrecy: bob_responder_secrecy* Table 2, Authentication: authentication* Table 2, PQXDH Theorem 1 corollaries: peer_authentication, handshake_forward_secrecy, kci_resistance, session_independence, hndl_resistance\n\nDeclarations on this page (10)theorem forward_secrecytheorem post_compromise_securitytheorem alice_initiator_secrecytheorem bob_responder_secrecytheorem authenticationtheorem peer_authenticationtheorem handshake_forward_secrecytheorem kci_resistancetheorem session_independencetheorem hndl_resistance\n\n\n\nProtocol Security Theorems in the Composed Symbolic ModelTrusted: the statement.Proves the ten symbolic security properties (Symbolic/Specifications/Security/Model.lean) over every reachable trace of the composed PQXDH ∘ SPQR ∘ Triple Ratchet DyLean model (Symbolic/Specifications/Model/Instance.lean), with unbounded sessions and participants, optional one-time prekeys, and dynamic state and primitive compromises:* Ratchet security (forward_secrecy, post_compromise_security), extending Cheval, Jacomme & Richards (IEEE S&P 2026) to the composed protocol from message 0 onward.* Handshake security (alice_initiator_secrecy, bob_responder_secrecy, authentication, and five corollaries), lifting Theorems 7–9 and Theorem 1 of Bhargavan et al. (USENIX Security 2024) into the composed protocol with per-block session-state leak excuses (SessionStateExposed).Role names follow libsignal: Alice fetches Bob's prekey bundle and sends first (AliceDone), while Bob publishes the bundle and receives first (BobDone). Symbolic/Theorems/Security.lean lifts all ten theorems to the compiled binary via Symbolic/Theorems/RefinementPreservation.lean.\n\n\n\ntheorem forward_secrecyL43–48 ↗#Forward secrecy (Security.ForwardSecrecy; Cheval et al., 2026, Table 2): every message key used on a reachable trace is unknown to the attacker unless that message key, an ancestor chain key, or an ancestor root key (including an initial root or chain key whose producing handshakes are all excused) was compromised. Exposing SPQR state (ExposedSpqr) does not excuse message-key recovery.\n\n\n\ntheorem post_compromise_securityL56–61 ↗#Post-compromise security (Security.PostCompromiseSecurity; Cheval et al., 2026, Table 2): for any message key derived at or after a DH ratchet step between honest DH keys, an earlier root-key compromise excuses attacker knowledge only if one of the two healing DH secrets was compromised or a post-step root, chain, or message key was leaked.\n\n\n\ntheorem alice_initiator_secrecyL69–74 ↗#Secrecy of Alice's PQXDH handshake keys (Security.AliceInitiatorSecrecy; Bhargavan et al., 2024, Theorem 7): when Alice (AliceDone) initiates a session, each output block (rk, ck, pqr) is unknown to the attacker unless AliceHandshakeWasCompromised holds or that block was leaked from session state (SessionStateExposed).\n\ntheorem bob_responder_secrecyL76–81 ↗#Secrecy of Bob's PQXDH handshake keys (Security.BobResponderSecrecy; Bhargavan et al., 2024, Theorem 9): when Bob (BobDone) accepts a session from Alice, each output block (rk, ck, pqr) is unknown to the attacker unless BobHandshakeWasCompromised holds or that block was leaked from session state (SessionStateExposed).\n\ntheorem authenticationL83–89 ↗#Handshake authentication of Alice to Bob (Security.Authentication; Bhargavan et al., 2024, Theorem 8): whenever Bob (BobDone) accepts a handshake from Alice, Alice previously completed a matching handshake (ResponderSessionAgreement, agreeing on pqpk and on spk and opk up to canonical X25519 decoding) unless Alice's identity key, Bob's session signed prekey, or DH was compromised beforehand.\n\ntheorem peer_authenticationL91–95 ↗#Peer authentication (Security.PeerAuthentication; Bhargavan et al., 2024, Theorem 1): if Bob accepts a handshake from Alice and neither Alice's identity key, Bob's session signed prekey, nor DH was compromised beforehand, then Alice previously completed a matching handshake.\n\ntheorem handshake_forward_secrecyL97–102 ↗#Handshake forward secrecy (Security.HandshakeForwardSecrecy; Bhargavan et al., 2024, Theorem 1): compromising either party's identity key after handshake completion reveals no unexposed handshake key block (even if one-time prekeys are compromised), provided Bob's session signed and Kyber prekeys are uncompromised and DH and KEM remain unbroken.\n\ntheorem kci_resistanceL104–108 ↗#Key-compromise-impersonation resistance (Security.KCIResistance; Bhargavan et al., 2024, Theorem 1): compromising Bob's identity key does not enable impersonating Alice to Bob unless Alice's identity key, Bob's session signed prekey, or DH was compromised before Bob accepted.\n\ntheorem session_independenceL110–115 ↗#Session independence (Security.SessionIndependence; Bhargavan et al., 2024, Theorem 1): compromising signed, Kyber, or one-time prekeys outside the target session reveals no unexposed handshake key block when both parties' identity keys are uncompromised and DH and KEM remain unbroken.\n\ntheorem hndl_resistanceL117–125 ↗#Harvest-now-decrypt-later resistance (Security.HNDLResistance; Bhargavan et al., 2024, Theorem 1): breaking classical DH (BrokenDH) after handshake completion reveals no unexposed handshake key block when KEM hardness holds and the session's identity, signed, and Kyber prekeys are uncompromised.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Symbolic/Theorems/ModelSecurity.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Theorem-files/Symbolic.Theorems.ModelSecurity/#file-Symbolic___Theorems___ModelSecurity"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalSpqrV1.MlKemCorrect/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalSpqrV1___MlKemCorrect":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalSpqrV1 › MlKemCorrect.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 1 headline theorem depend on it 19 of 63 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nSPQR V1: ML-KEM-768 δ-correctness (MlKem.CorrectWith)Trusted in part. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use MlKem.honestExchangeFails from this file; CorrectWith is checked by Lean (see Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md).Defines the single-exchange failure predicate MlKem.honestExchangeFails over the incremental ML-KEM wrappers (generate, encaps1, ek_matches_header, encaps2 with the issue-1275 fix-up, and decaps in States.lean), and the average-case δ-correctness hypothesis MlKem.CorrectWith over uniform 64-byte keygen seed and 32-byte encapsulation randomness (FIPS 203 §3.2, Table 1; DEV-SPQR6).\n\n\n\ndef MlKem.honestExchangeFailsL33–51 ↗#Returns true when one honest SPQR ML-KEM exchange fails on 64-byte keygen seed and 32-byte encapsulation randomness m: any wrapper (generate, encaps1, encaps2, decaps) errors, ek_matches_header rejects the generated ek, or the decapsulated secret differs from the encapsulated one.\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 53–64): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.LibsignalSpqrV1.MlKem.CorrectWith\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalSpqrV1/MlKemCorrect.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalSpqrV1.MlKemCorrect/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalSpqrV1___MlKemCorrect"}});